page wireframe { sites = [:showcase]
  h1 "Wireframe widgets"

  text {
    span "Wireframe widgets mock up a UI from composable blocks. Each `wf_*` is a **diagram shape**, so it lives inside a `diagram` — windows and panels hold other widgets, layout blocks (`wf_row` / `wf_column` / `wf_grid`) arrange them, and you place the whole thing with `x` / `y` (or anchors) and connect widgets with edges. Back to [the overview](overview)."
  }

  h2 "A sign-in window"

  // A `wf_window` is a container shape: its child widgets render in its
  // body (stacked vertically by default). Drop a `wf_row` / `wf_grid` to
  // arrange controls differently, and a `wf_panel` to group them. The
  // window is sized by its content; the diagram just frames it.
  diagram {
    width = 300  height = 430
    wf_window "Sign in" {
      wf_label "Email"
      wf_input "you@example.com"

      wf_label "Account type"
      wf_dropdown "Personal"

      wf_panel {
        title = "Billing plan"
        wf_row {
          wf_radio "Monthly" { selected = true }
          wf_radio "Annual"
        }
      }

      wf_checkbox "Remember me" { checked = true }
      wf_toggle "Email notifications" { on = true }

      // A two-column grid of footer buttons.
      wf_grid {
        columns = 2
        wf_button "Cancel"
        wf_button "Sign in" { icon = "lucide.check" }
      }
    }
  }

  h2 "Device frames"

  text {
    span "Beyond the bare window, the family ships device shells — `wf_browser`, `wf_phone`, `wf_tablet` — that hold the same widgets. They have a realistic fixed default size (so content sizes inside them properly); set `orientation = :landscape` on a phone / tablet to rotate it, or pin either axis with `width` / `height`."
  }

  // A browser frame: a toolbar (traffic-light dots + address bar URL) over a
  // content area. Nest a window or any controls inside.
  diagram {
    width = 480  height = 240
    wf_browser "app.example.com" {
      wf_panel { title = "Dashboard"
        wf_input "Search projects…"
        wf_row {
          wf_button "Open"
          wf_button "New" { icon = "lucide.check" }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // A phone (portrait) and a tablet (landscape) side by side.
  diagram {
    width = 640  height = 480
    wf_phone "9:41" {
      x = 0.0  y = 0.0
      wf_panel { title = "Account"
        wf_input "Email" { value = "ada@example.com" }
        wf_toggle "Notifications" { on = true }
      }
      wf_button "Sign in" { icon = "lucide.check" }
    }
    wf_tablet {
      x = 300.0  y = 0.0
      orientation = :landscape
      wf_row {
        wf_panel { title = "Library"
          wf_label "Recent"
          wf_label "Favourites"
        }
        wf_panel { title = "Reader"
          wf_label "Select an item to read."
        }
      }
    }
  }

  h2 "Placing widgets and connecting them"

  text {
    span "Because widgets are diagram shapes, you place them with `x` / `y` and draw edges between them by `id` — the same way you connect any two shapes. Here a launcher button flows to the window it opens."
  }

  diagram {
    width = 360  height = 200
    wf_button "Open settings" { id = launch  x = 20.0  y = 80.0 }
    wf_window "Settings" {
      id = win  x = 180.0  y = 20.0
      wf_checkbox "Dark mode" { checked = true }
      wf_button "Close"
    }
    launch -> win
  }

  h2 "Node graphs"

  text {
    span "A `wf_node_graph` mocks up a node editor — a shader graph, blueprint, or dataflow pipeline. Each `wf_node` is a box with labeled `inputs` (left edge) and `outputs` (right edge); a `wf_link` wires one node's output port to another's input port via `\"node.port\"`. Nodes auto-lay-out left-to-right from the links (the same layered solver diagrams use), and links route with the orthogonal edge router. Give a node an explicit `x` / `y` to pin it."
  }

  diagram {
    width = 560  height = 240
    wf_node_graph {
      wf_node "Texture" {
        id = tex
        outputs = ["RGB", "Alpha"]
      }
      wf_node "Multiply" {
        id = mul
        inputs  = ["A", "B"]
        outputs = ["Result"]
      }
      wf_node "Fresnel" {
        id = fres
        outputs = ["Factor"]
      }
      wf_node "Output" {
        id = out
        inputs = ["Color"]
      }
      wf_link "tex.RGB"    { to = "mul.A" }
      wf_link "fres.Factor" { to = "mul.B" }
      wf_link "mul.Result" { to = "out.Color" }
    }
  }

  h2 "A separate UI theme"

  text {
    span "Wireframes mock up an application, so they follow a UI theme that's separate from the document's. Set it per site (`site { ui_theme = :gruvbox  ui_mode = :light }`), or override it on any widget with its own `theme` / `accent` / `mode` — this window is forced to Gruvbox light regardless of the page theme."
  }

  diagram {
    width = 300  height = 130
    wf_window "Gruvbox light" {
      theme = :gruvbox
      mode  = :light
      wf_label "A self-contained app theme"
      wf_row {
        wf_checkbox "Enabled" { checked = true }
        wf_toggle "Sync" { on = true }
      }
      wf_button "Save"
    }
  }

  h2 "Per-widget class overrides"

  text {
    span "A widget's class `fill` / `stroke` paint and raw `color` declaration are read and baked onto its box and label — add one to a single instance (the primary button carries `class = [\"primary\"]`)."
  }

  diagram {
    width = 300  height = 44
    wf_row {
      wf_button "Default"
      wf_button "Primary" { class = ["primary"] }
      wf_button "Disabled" { disabled = true }
    }
  }
}

// Recolour the primary button through the class system. The widget reads
// its class `fill` / `stroke` and raw `color` declaration and bakes them onto
// the box and label;
// the `dark` / `light` blocks adapt it to the reader's theme.
class primary {
  fill   = "#1f6feb"
  stroke = "#1f6feb"
  css    = "color:#ffffff;"
  dark  { fill = "#388bfd"  stroke = "#388bfd" }
  light { fill = "#0969da"  stroke = "#0969da" }
}
